// **A sprawling fruiting habit: short determinate axes, repeated, each one
// ending in a truss.** The organ package's second authored habit, and
// deliberately unlike `herb.ron` on every axis that composes a silhouette --
// two organ species that read as one plant in two sizes would fail this phase
// exactly as the four woody species did.
//
// **A category rather than a particular plant**, for the reason `herb.ron`'s
// header sets out: everything already in the set names a *kind* of plant, at
// whatever level of specificity was convenient, and never one real organism.
// `Reports/plant-morphology-evolvability-2026-08-26.md` §6 rules directly
// against portraits of named plants as the artifact.
//
// **This name is the weaker of the two and it is worth saying so.** A bramble
// is *Rubus*, which is a genus -- so it sits at `conifer`'s level of
// specificity rather than `shrub`'s, and it is closer to a named plant than
// `herb` is. It is kept because the vernacular is genuinely used for any
// sprawling thorny fruiting thicket-former rather than for one species, and
// because renaming a file twice costs more than it buys. The strict
// growth-form word for this habit is *scrambler* -- a plant that clambers
// over its neighbours without true climbing organs -- and that is the name to
// take if the distinction is ever tightened.
//
// **Sympody is what carries it, and this engine has had sympody as a
// heritable allele since Phase 2** (`plant-morphology-reach-2026-08-23.md`
// §2). Each axis makes a few metamers, terminates, and a lateral already
// deposited below it takes over as the new leader. Until organs existed it
// terminated in *nothing*, so the mechanism fired, was counted, and looked
// like an ordinary shrub -- the fourth of the five invisible levers. What it
// terminates in now is a **truss**: a small cluster of flowers that sets
// fruit.
//
//                    herb                   bramble
//   habit            one erect leader       sympodial, sprawling
//   determinacy      8 metamers, once       3 metamers, over and over
//   organs           one big terminal head  many small trusses
//   flower colour    yellow / orange        white / violet
//   fruit colour     gold seed head         red and crimson
//   leaves           8-cell sprays, sparse  6-cell sprays, dense
(
    name: "bramble",
    // Mid green, like a tree's -- the *foliage* is deliberately not what
    // separates these two, because a foliage-only difference is the
    // colour-only distinction the owner has already rejected three times.
    foliage_bands: (first: 2, count: 2),
    // Pale herbaceous stem, as `herb`.
    bark_bands: (first: 6, count: 2),
    // **White and violet**, bands 6-7 -- and the range is deliberately as
    // far as it goes from `herb`'s 0-1. Two organ species drawing
    // neighbouring bands would be the "biggest differences are still size
    // and colour" verdict arriving through the one channel this phase adds.
    flower_bands: (first: 6, count: 2),
    // Red and crimson: bands 0-1 of `fruit`, the loudest it has.
    fruit_bands: (first: 0, count: 2),
    windfall_material: "windfall",
    stomatal_reserve: 0.15,
    seed_half_life: 16000.0,
    remains_half_life: 600.0,
    fates: [
        (GrowingTip, [
            // **Three metamers and the axis stops in a truss.** The number
            // is the whole difference in habit between this and `herb`: a
            // short determinate unit, repeated, is a bush; one long one is a
            // stalk. Nothing else in the growth model differs.
            //
            // **What carries the plant on is sympody**, not this rule: the
            // apex is consumed by the flowers, and a lateral already
            // deposited below it becomes the new leader. That relay is the
            // mechanism `LOCUS_SYMPODIAL` has expressed since Phase 2 with
            // nothing to relay *to*.
            (when: Node,  becomes: Flower, after_metamers: Some(3)),
            (when: Node,  becomes: DormantBud, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(GrowingTip), lateral: Some(GrowingTip)),
            // As `herb`: an axis that starved short of its count gets
            // no truss. Otherwise the count would be decorative.
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            (when: Grew,  becomes: MatureBody, child: Some(RootTip), lateral: Some(RootTip)),
            (when: Stale, becomes: MatureBody),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            (when: Flush, becomes: GrowingTip),
        ]),
        (Flower, [
            (when: Ripe, becomes: Fruit),
        ]),
        (Fruit, [
            (when: Ripe, becomes: Seed),
        ]),
    ],
    cell_types: [
        (Seed, [
            Germinate(
                light_threshold: 0.08,
                soil_water_threshold: 0.18,
                instant: false,
            ),
        ]),
        (GrowingTip, [
            Grow(
                cost: 0.14,
                // **Branches freely**, where `herb` has none at all. With
                // a three-metamer determinate unit, the laterals *are* the
                // plant: every one of them is the next relay.
                branch_chance: [0.35, 0.4],
                continuation_weight: 0.6,
                light_weight: [0.3, 0.35],
                wind_weight: 0.25,
                // **Falls away sharply with order**, which is what arching
                // means: the leader stands, the side axes lean out. This is
                // the single number that most separates its outline from
                // `herb`'s -- and the first pass had it at [0.4, 0.15],
                // which was not a bush but a mat: measured on a six-plant
                // sheet the stand merged into one continuous ground layer
                // across the whole world, which is the owner's standing
                // "everything has merged together into a big mass"
                // complaint arriving through a new species.
                upward_weight: [0.75, 0.4],
                crowding_weight: 30.0,
                max_active_tips: 14,
                // A node every two cells, so a three-metamer axis is about
                // six cells long -- short, which is what makes the habit
                // bushy rather than open.
                plastochron: [2, 2],
                penetration_force: 0.0,
                // A low ceiling. This plant sprawls at knee height.
                turgor_source: 0.3,
                turgor_yield: 0.1,
                turgor_per_cell: 0.007,
                turgor_taper: 0.4,
                // Floppy, against `herb`'s 0.93.
                heading_inertia: 0.55,
                branch_angle: [65.0, 60.0],
                internode: [2, 2],
                leaf_cluster: 5,
                // **A truss, not a head**: four cells, and there are many
                // of them. Against `herb`'s one nine-cell head, this is the
                // same organ machinery producing an entirely different
                // distribution over the plant.
                organ_cluster: 5,
                juvenile_size: 10,
                juvenile_plastochron: 0.7,
                juvenile_branch: 1.2,
                genotype_variance: (0.5, 0.0, 0.4, 0.18, 0.7, 0.0, 0.4, 0.5, 0.0, 0.7),
                // **Sympodial**, which is the whole habit -- see the fate
                // table above. The species value is the *centre* of the
                // founding draw, so a stand carries a monopodial minority.
                sympodial: [true],
                tropism: [Orthotropic, Plagiotropic],
            ),
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.5, shade_death: 0.0, drought_death: 0.0),
        ]),
        (RootTip, [
            Absorb(rate: 1.5),
            Grow(
                cost: 0.16,
                branch_chance: [0.4],
                continuation_weight: 0.6,
                light_weight: [0.0],
                wind_weight: 0.0,
                upward_weight: [0.3],
                crowding_weight: 0.0,
                max_active_tips: 10,
                plastochron: [0],
                heading_inertia: 0.45,
                penetration_force: 1.0,
                genotype_variance: (0.0, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.25, 0.0),
            ),
        ]),
        (MatureBody, [
            Reproduce(seed_cost: 0.16, reproductive_allocation: 0.30, seed_maturity: 50),
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 16.0),
            StructuralAnchor,
            Absorb(rate: 1.5),
        ]),
        (DormantBud, [
            BudBreak(
                cost: 0.14,
                thickening_survival: 0.5,
                // **Basitonous**, against `herb`'s +1.8. What breaks is
                // low down, which is the other half of "bush, not stalk" --
                // Barthelemy & Caraglio's two phenomena, and the shrub file
                // already uses the negative arm for the same reason.
                acrotony: -1.4,
            ),
            StructuralAnchor,
            SecondaryThicken(pipe_ratio: 16.0),
        ]),
        (Leaf, [
            Photosynthesize(rate: 0.4, shade_death: 0.001, drought_death: 0.001),
        ]),
        (Flower, [
            // Faster to *set* than `herb`, slower to ripen. The set rate is
            // what makes a bramble carry flowers and fruit at once; the ripen
            // rate is how long the fruit is on the plant to be *seen*, and
            // the first pass had it at 0.02 -- fruit that appeared and fell
            // inside 50 ticks, so a contact sheet of a fruiting stand showed
            // flowers and no fruit at all. What is on the plant is the half
            // a picture can judge. This habit turns over
            // trusses continuously where an erect determinate herb spends its
            // whole season on one head, and that difference is visible on a
            // sheet: a bramble carries flowers and fruit at once.
            Ripen(rate: 0.035, cost: 0.22),
        ]),
        (Fruit, [
            Ripen(rate: 0.011, cost: 0.28),
        ]),
    ],
)
